//? Title: Bayesian Optimization with Expected Improvement
//? Subtitle: Twelve sequential choices on a fixed candidate grid
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

\abstract(
Minimize a multimodal function with 15 objective evaluations. A Gaussian
process models the observations, and expected improvement chooses each
new point from a 201-point grid.
)
\toc

> Choosing an evaluation

The surrogate has zero prior mean, unit signal variance and an RBF covariance
with length scale .24. A diagonal term of $10^{-8}$ stabilizes conditioning.
After each evaluation the report conditions the GP on all available observations.

For predictive mean $\mu$, latent standard deviation $s$, best observed value
$b$ and margin $\xi=.005$, the improvement is $\max(b-\xi-F,0)$ where
$F\sim\mathrm{Normal}(\mu,s^2)$. Its expectation is

$$
\mathrm{EI}=g\Phi(g/s)+s\phi(g/s),\qquad g=b-\xi-\mu.
$$
\alttext(Expected improvement combines the gain weighted by the probability of improvement and a term proportional to the predictive standard deviation.)

The optimizer evaluates the first unseen point with the largest EI. Posterior
uncertainty can give an unseen point high EI even when its mean is above the
incumbent.

> Fit the surrogate and search

Start at -1, 0 and 1. Each of the next twelve rounds saves posterior means,
latent variances and acquisitions for all 201 points. The dense objective curve
and grid optimum are calculated after the sequential search to assess the final
grid regret.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/bayesian_optimization_trace.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  constexpr int size = 201, rounds = 12;
  const auto objective = [](double x) {
    const double displacement = x - .3;
    return std::sin(8. * x) + .35 * std::cos(17. * x) + .22 * displacement * displacement;
  };
  std::vector<double> grid(size);
  for (int i = 0; i < size; ++i)
    grid[i] = -1. + 2. * i / (size - 1);
  dm::optim::RBFGaussianProcessSurrogate gp(.24, 1e-8);
  std::vector<int> indices;
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> points;
  std::vector<double> measured, values, incumbent, trace, last_mean, last_variance;
  std::vector<bool> visited(size);
  const auto observe = [&](int index) {
    if (index < 0 || visited[index])
      throw std::runtime_error("Expected an unseen grid point");
    visited[index] = true;
    indices.push_back(index);
    measured.push_back(grid[index]);
    points.push_back({grid[index]});
    values.push_back(objective(grid[index]));
    incumbent.push_back(*std::ranges::min_element(values));
  };
  for (int i : {0, size / 2, size - 1})
    observe(i);

  for (int round = 0; round < rounds; ++round) {
    gp.fit(points, values);
    std::vector<double> mean, variance, acquisition;
    int choice = -1;
    double best_score = -1.;
    for (int i = 0; i < size; ++i) {
      const auto prediction = gp.predict({grid[i]});
      const double ei = dm::optim::expected_improvement(
          prediction.mean, prediction.sd, incumbent.back(), .005);
      if (!std::isfinite(ei))
        throw std::runtime_error("Expected finite improvement");
      mean.push_back(prediction.mean);
      variance.push_back(prediction.sd * prediction.sd);
      acquisition.push_back(ei);
      if (!visited[i] && ei > best_score) {
        choice = i;
        best_score = ei;
      }
    }
    for (const auto *series : {&mean, &variance, &acquisition})
      trace.insert(trace.end(), series->begin(), series->end());
    last_mean = std::move(mean);
    last_variance = std::move(variance);
    observe(choice);
  }

  std::vector<double> truth, lower, upper;
  for (int i = 0; i < size; ++i) {
    truth.push_back(objective(grid[i]));
    const double margin = 1.96 * std::sqrt(last_variance[i]);
    lower.push_back(last_mean[i] - margin);
    upper.push_back(last_mean[i] + margin);
  }
  auto band_x = grid;
  band_x.insert(band_x.end(), grid.rbegin(), grid.rend());
  lower.insert(lower.end(), upper.rbegin(), upper.rend());
  auto fit = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  fit.polygon(band_x, lower, dm::RGBA{37, 99, 235, .16})
      .lines(grid, truth, "objective (audit)", {107, 114, 128})
      .lines(grid, last_mean, "GP mean before final evaluation", {37, 99, 235})
      .points(measured, values, "observations", {194, 65, 12})
      .title("Final acquisition round").x_label("x").y_label("objective");
  auto convergence = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  convergence.lines(n::arange(1, 16).to_vector<double>(), incumbent, "best observed", {15, 118, 110})
      .title("Incumbent after each objective evaluation")
      .x_label("evaluation number").y_label("best objective value");
  const double regret = incumbent.back() - *std::ranges::min_element(truth);
  if (incumbent.back() >= incumbent.at(2) || regret > 1e-8)
    throw std::runtime_error("Search did not improve and find the grid optimum");
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2).add(fit).add(convergence).fit_panels()
      .size(1150, 460).save_svg("build/bayesian_optimization_trace.svg");
  n::save_npz("build/bayesian_optimization_trace.npz",
      {{"grid", n::from_vector(grid, {size, 1})}, {"indices", n::from_vector(indices)},
       {"observations", n::from_vector(values)},
       {"mean_variance_ei", n::from_vector(trace, {rounds, 3, size})}});
  std::println("{} evaluations. Initial incumbent {:.6f}, final incumbent {:.6f}, grid regret {:.6g}.",
               values.size(), incumbent.at(2), incumbent.back(), regret);
  std::println("Evaluation grid indices {}.", indices);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
15 evaluations. Initial incumbent -0.713865, final incumbent -1.310135, grid regret 0.
Evaluation grid indices [0, 100, 200, 26, 145, 164, 70, 160, 157, 83, 9, 80, 190, 1, 2].
\image(figures/bayesian_optimization_trace.svg)
// result_end
\alttext(The Gaussian process mean tracks the multimodal objective near sampled inputs. The best observation falls over successive evaluations.)

> References

- [Jones, Schonlau and Welch (1998), Efficient Global Optimization of Expensive Black-Box Functions](https://doi.org/10.1023/A:1008306431147).
